# Introduction and package description

This is Non-compartment anlysis software. The package is designed for batch processing of pharmacokinetic data.

See documentation:

  * Dev: [https://pharmcat.github.io/MetidaNCA.jl/dev/](https://pharmcat.github.io/MetidaNCA.jl/dev/)
  * Stable: [https://pharmcat.github.io/MetidaNCA.jl/stable/](https://pharmcat.github.io/MetidaNCA.jl/stable/)

## Validation purpose

The main validation purpose is confirmation by examination and provision of objective evidence that software
specifications conform to user needs and intended uses, and that the particular requirements
implemented through software can be consistently fulfilled.

## Acceptance criteria

### Kinds of evidence

Comparisons in this report rest on three kinds of reference, and they are not
interchangeable. The chapter *Validation coverage* reports, for every parameter and every
capability, which of them applies.

  * **External** — the corresponding value from an independent implementation, Phoenix
    WinNonlin 8.0 (see Appendix 2). This is the evidence for most of the report.
  * **Analytic** — a closed-form value. Where it applies it is stronger than any comparison
    with another program, because the reference is exact and belongs to no implementation.
    The chapter on the integration rules rests on it.
  * **Internal** — a value this package produced and that is carried forward unchanged. It
    establishes that behaviour has not changed and **nothing more**; it is not evidence of
    correctness. It is used only where no external reference can be obtained, which in this
    report means the `:luldt` integration rule, for which Phoenix implements no counterpart.
    Wherever an internal baseline is used, the report says so at the point of use.

Graphics are qualified operationally: the figure is reproduced for visual verification and
the data it carries is compared with the data the package computed.

### The criterion

A single comparison is accepted when

$|x_{MetidaNCA} - x_{ref}| \le r_{tol} \times |x_{ref}| + a_{tol}$

with $r_{tol}$ = `j ACCEPT_RTOL` and $a_{tol}$ = `j ACCEPT_ATOL`, that is, agreement to four
significant digits. The same criterion is applied to all three kinds of reference, which is
conservative in different ways for each. External reference values are transcribed with six
significant digits, so the attainable agreement there is bounded by the transcription of the
reference rather than by the computation; the largest deviation observed over the external
scenarios of this report is of the order of $10^{-7}$, leaving about three orders of
magnitude of margin. Against a closed form the bound is machine precision instead, and the
deviations observed are of the order of $10^{-16}$ — the criterion is loose there by twelve
orders of magnitude, and the agreement actually attained is reported in the tables of the
integration-rules chapter. The checks on figures demand exact equality, since a plot carries
the computed numbers and does not round them.

The report is accepted only when **all** of the following hold:

  * every comparison is within the criteria above;
  * the package test suite passes (see Testing results);
  * every required scenario reported its results.

The third condition is not redundant: the document generator renders a failing code chunk
into the document and carries on, which would otherwise drop a results table without any
other sign. The same applies to the Conclusion, which marks itself once it has rendered.

### Where the verdict is

This report is produced whatever the outcome of the comparisons, and it carries its own
verdict. Each comparison is recorded, as it is made, in
`docs/src/validation_acceptance.tsv`. The Conclusion section states the verdict, summarises
every scenario and lists **every divergence** — parameter, subject, computed value,
reference value, difference, relative error and the criterion it was judged against. The
same journal is written next to this report as `docs/src/validation_divergences.tsv`, so it
can be read without opening the PDF. An empty journal is itself a statement: the file is
written even when nothing diverged.

Independently of the report, `acceptance_verdict()` in `docs/acceptance.jl` is called
outside the document generator, after the report is built, and aborts the documentation
build on a negative verdict — the document generator swallows errors raised inside a code
chunk, so a verdict computed only inside the report could never fail. Running the build
with `VALIDATION_STRICT=false` suppresses the abort and leaves the report for inspection;
the verdict and the journal are unaffected by that setting.

## Requirements

  * Julia 1.8 (or higher) installed for Operating System/OS Version/Architecture in Tier 1 list

  *Tier 1: Julia is guaranteed to build from source and pass all tests on these platforms when built with the default options. 
  Official binaries are always available and CI is run on every commit to ensure support is actively maintained.*


## Developer software life cycle

  * Development stage
  * Testing procedures development
  * Performing testing procedures on local machine
  * Push to development branch
  * Make pull request to main branch
  * Performing testing procedures with GitHub Actions
  * Make pull request to the official registry of general Julia packages (if nessesary)
  * Make release (if previous completed)

### Versions

  * X.Y.Z - patch release (no breaking changes)
  * X.Y.0 - minor release (may include breaking changes if X = 0)
  * X.0.0 - major release (breaking changes, changes in public API)
  * 0.#.# - no stable public API
  * 1.#.# or higher - stable public API

## Build support

### Tier 1

  * julia-version: 1.8, 1
  * julia-arch: x64
  * os: ubuntu-latest, macOS-latest, windows-latest

